<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helvetii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Helvetians, Elouetioi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Helvetii were a Celtic tribe or tribal confederation that occupied most of the Swiss Plateau when they came into contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in the 1st century BCE. They had no kings but seem to have been ruled by a class of noblemen. In 58 BCE they burned their homes and set out to settle in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, a migration that prompted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s conquest of Gaul; defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>, they were sent back to rebuild their homes, and Caesar's own figures, which modern scholars think too high, claim only about 30 percent survived. Subjugated after 52 BCE and largely Romanised by the 2nd century, they were later assimilated by the Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> the Helvetii were divided into four subgroups, of which he names the Verbigeni and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigurini">Tigurini</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> names the Tigurini and the Tougeni. They had no kings at the time of their clash with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> but seem to have been governed by a class of noblemen. The earliest attestation of the name is a graffito of about 300 BCE from Mantua, in Etruscan letters. Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> place the Helvetii originally in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and their move to the Swiss Plateau is usually placed in the late 2nd century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigurini">Tigurini</a> joined the raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a>, and in 107 BCE they killed the consul L. Cassius Longinus at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>. In 58 BCE, after the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orgetorix">Orgetorix</a>, who had planned a new migration, died before he could be tried, the Helvetii burned their homes and set out with the Raurici, Latobrigi, Tulingi and a group of Boii, intending to settle among the Santones. Caesar closed the bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> against them and attacked the part of their force still on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>. He then caught them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>, where they retreated and later surrendered.</p><p>Caesar allowed the Helvetii to return and rebuild their homes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> later counted them among the allied tribes bound by treaty. Caesar reports a total of 368,000 people, of whom 263,000 were Helvetii, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 110,000 survivors. Modern scholars think these figures far too high: only one of fifteen Celtic oppida in their territory shows destruction by fire, and Furger-Gunti estimates about 160,000 emigrants and 40,000 warriors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Helvetii were subjugated after 52 BCE. Under Augustus Celtic oppida such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindonissa">Vindonissa</a> and Basilea became Roman garrisons, and a Helvetian uprising was crushed in 68 CE by Aulus Caecina Alienus. The Swiss Plateau was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a> in 22 BCE and into Germania Superior in 83 CE, and the Helvetii were largely Romanised by the 2nd century. In the later 3rd century Roman control weakened and the Alemanni invaded. The Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> settled permanently in the 5th and 6th centuries, and the Helvetii were largely assimilated, contributing to the origin of the modern Swiss people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helvetii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lepontic in northern Italy used an alphabet adapted from the Etruscan, Celtiberian was written in the Iberian script, and in southern Gaul Gaulish was written in Greek letters; Caesar found lists of the <b>Helvetii</b> written in Greek letters.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Each tribe had a council of elders and at first a king; by Caesar’s day several, such as the Aedui and the <b>Helvetii</b>, were governed instead by magistrates elected every year.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
